1
00:00:00,910 --> 00:00:07,160
[Williams] And during our phone conversation we
heard a, a gas level being announced from

2
00:00:07,210 --> 00:00:08,120
Sperry Sun.

3
00:00:08,170 --> 00:00:11,800
My wife actually heard the announcement through
the telephone, through the speaker,

4
00:00:11,850 --> 00:00:14,280
and asked if I needed to get off the
phone to go take care of it.

5
00:00:14,330 --> 00:00:16,180
And I told her no, it was
just an indication

6
00:00:16,770 --> 00:00:17,760
that,

7
00:00:17,810 --> 00:00:20,120
uh, to make everyone aware of what
the gas levels are.

8
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:25,050
That was Mike Williams, the chief electronics
technician on the Deepwater Horizon,

9
00:00:25,100 --> 00:00:27,250
testifying three months later.

10
00:00:27,300 --> 00:00:31,310
The call he described was on the night
of April 20, twenty ten.

11
00:00:31,560 --> 00:00:36,390
The rig was drilling in the Gulf of Mexico,
about fifty miles off Louisiana.

12
00:00:36,440 --> 00:00:39,650
126 people were on board.

13
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:44,330
Earlier that evening, the crew had tested
whether the well was sealed.

14
00:00:44,680 --> 00:00:46,950
The readings contradicted each other.

15
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:49,110
One line showed no flow.

16
00:00:49,160 --> 00:00:54,530
The drill pipe, open to the same
well, showed 1,400 psi.

17
00:00:54,880 --> 00:00:58,650
Around eight o'clock, the test was
declared a success.

18
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:02,870
The presidential commission later concluded that
the pressure could only have come

19
00:01:02,920 --> 00:01:07,410
from a leak into the well, and called
the decision a key error.

20
00:01:07,860 --> 00:01:12,990
Williams told investigators why the gas
announcement that night did not alarm him.

21
00:01:13,160 --> 00:01:16,870
[Williams] We had gotten them so frequently that I'd
actually become somewhat immune to them.

22
00:01:17,360 --> 00:01:21,110
I, I'd get to the point where I didn't even hear
them anymore, uh, especially with

23
00:01:21,160 --> 00:01:23,470
this well, because we were getting
gas back continuously.

24
00:01:23,960 --> 00:01:26,990
By his account, the call was around 9:30.

25
00:01:27,040 --> 00:01:31,510
At about 9:40, mud and gas burst
onto the rig floor.

26
00:01:31,560 --> 00:01:34,450
Minutes later, the rig exploded.

27
00:01:35,800 --> 00:01:38,050
Eleven men were killed.

28
00:01:42,400 --> 00:01:47,470
Investigators would spend years asking how the
well had been judged safe, and why

29
00:01:47,520 --> 00:01:50,970
the blowout preventer on the
seafloor did not stop it.

30
00:01:56,433 --> 00:02:01,183
The Deepwater Horizon was a floating drilling
rig, owned by Transocean and hired by

31
00:02:01,233 --> 00:02:02,233
BP.

32
00:02:02,553 --> 00:02:05,970
Below it lay about 5,000 feet of water.

33
00:02:06,020 --> 00:02:10,583
A pipe called the riser ran down to the
seafloor, to the blowout preventer: a

34
00:02:10,633 --> 00:02:15,490
360-ton stack of valves and steel
blades on top of the well.

35
00:02:15,540 --> 00:02:20,083
From there, the well went down another two
and a half miles into the rock.

36
00:02:20,433 --> 00:02:26,436
The well was about 38 days behind schedule
and some 58 million over budget.

37
00:02:26,486 --> 00:02:31,163
In an email that April, a BP engineer
called it a nightmare well.

38
00:02:31,513 --> 00:02:35,623
126 people from thirteen
companies were aboard.

39
00:02:35,673 --> 00:02:39,436
BP's well site leaders were Bob
Kaluza and Don Vidrine.

40
00:02:39,486 --> 00:02:43,543
On the drill floor that evening were
Transocean's toolpusher Jason Anderson and

41
00:02:43,593 --> 00:02:45,523
driller Dewey Revette.

42
00:02:46,773 --> 00:02:50,643
Halliburton had cemented the bottom of
the well just after midnight.

43
00:02:50,693 --> 00:02:54,783
The plan now was to seal it
temporarily, and move the rig on.

44
00:02:55,633 --> 00:03:00,303
Three things stood between the oil and gas in
the rock and the rig above: the heavy

45
00:03:00,353 --> 00:03:04,383
drilling mud that filled the well, the cement
at the bottom, and the blowout

46
00:03:04,433 --> 00:03:05,563
preventer.

47
00:03:05,913 --> 00:03:10,183
To leave, the crew would replace the mud in the
riser and the top of the well with

48
00:03:10,233 --> 00:03:12,023
much lighter seawater.

49
00:03:12,073 --> 00:03:16,103
After that, the cement at the bottom would have
to hold back the reservoir on its

50
00:03:16,153 --> 00:03:17,283
own.

51
00:03:17,533 --> 00:03:21,830
That morning, BP had sent home a team that
could have checked the cement.

52
00:03:21,880 --> 00:03:24,816
Its decision plan said the
check was not needed.

53
00:03:24,866 --> 00:03:28,503
The commission later concluded that
BP should have run it.

54
00:03:28,953 --> 00:03:31,463
There was a warning in the
laboratory, too.

55
00:03:31,513 --> 00:03:35,950
Halliburton's own tests had shown the
cement mix to be unstable.

56
00:03:36,800 --> 00:03:40,910
The cement would be checked instead with a
negative pressure test: lower the

57
00:03:40,960 --> 00:03:44,590
pressure inside the well, as if the
rig had already gone.

58
00:03:44,640 --> 00:03:49,610
If the cement held, nothing would come
back: no pressure, and no flow.

59
00:03:49,960 --> 00:03:53,777
There was no standard procedure for
running or reading this test.

60
00:03:53,827 --> 00:03:58,130
And BP and Transocean had not formally
trained their people to do it.

61
00:03:58,480 --> 00:04:03,043
Starting around five p.m., the crew bled
the pressure off the drill pipe.

62
00:04:03,093 --> 00:04:04,430
It came back.

63
00:04:04,480 --> 00:04:08,890
After repeated attempts, it
built up to 1,400 psi.

64
00:04:09,240 --> 00:04:11,670
They discussed the readings
on the rig floor.

65
00:04:11,720 --> 00:04:15,430
According to statements from both well site
leaders, Jason Anderson said the

66
00:04:15,480 --> 00:04:20,390
pressure came from a so-called bladder effect:
heavy mud in the riser pressing down

67
00:04:20,440 --> 00:04:24,450
on a closed preventer, and pushing
pressure into the drill pipe.

68
00:04:24,800 --> 00:04:28,430
Anderson died that night and never
gave his own account.

69
00:04:28,480 --> 00:04:32,830
A federal court later found that no such effect
is known to exist in this kind of

70
00:04:32,880 --> 00:04:36,890
test, and questioned whether the idea
had come from him at all.

71
00:04:37,240 --> 00:04:41,990
Don Vidrine insisted on repeating the test on
the kill line, a narrow pipe from the

72
00:04:42,040 --> 00:04:44,290
rig down to the blowout preventer.

73
00:04:44,360 --> 00:04:46,870
Nothing flowed from it for 30 minutes.

74
00:04:46,920 --> 00:04:49,970
The drill pipe still read
fourteen hundred.

75
00:04:50,320 --> 00:04:54,250
Around eight p.m., the test was
declared a success.

76
00:04:54,600 --> 00:04:58,630
The commission later wrote that the
pressure data were not ambiguous.

77
00:04:58,680 --> 00:05:02,510
In its view, the men had started from the
assumption that the well could not be

78
00:05:02,560 --> 00:05:03,650
flowing.

79
00:05:04,100 --> 00:05:08,850
Four months earlier, a Transocean rig in the
North Sea had a near miss after a

80
00:05:08,900 --> 00:05:11,310
negative test was declared a success.

81
00:05:11,380 --> 00:05:16,030
According to Transocean, the lessons never
reached the Deepwater Horizon crew.

82
00:05:16,880 --> 00:05:21,390
At 8:02, the crew began pumping the
mud out and seawater in.

83
00:05:21,440 --> 00:05:25,670
Halliburton cementer Chris Haire was told the
test was good, and to get ready for

84
00:05:25,720 --> 00:05:27,690
the final cement plug.

85
00:05:28,040 --> 00:05:32,870
As the heavy mud left the well, so did the
weight holding back the reservoir.

86
00:05:32,920 --> 00:05:37,590
The cement at the bottom, investigators
later concluded, was not holding.

87
00:05:37,640 --> 00:05:42,770
According to later computer models, at about
8:50, oil and gas began to enter the

88
00:05:42,880 --> 00:05:43,870
well.

89
00:05:43,920 --> 00:05:49,150
At 9:01, the drill pipe pressure began to
rise while the pumps ran steadily.

90
00:05:49,200 --> 00:05:53,230
Minutes later, with the pumps off,
fluid kept flowing out.

91
00:05:53,280 --> 00:05:58,650
At about 9:10, the returns were sent overboard,
past the meters that measured them.

92
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:02,550
The crew was busy with other
work at the same time.

93
00:06:02,600 --> 00:06:05,570
Investigators concluded that the
signs were missed.

94
00:06:05,920 --> 00:06:11,790
Around 9:20, senior toolpusher Randy Ezell
phoned Jason Anderson on the rig floor.

95
00:06:11,840 --> 00:06:16,030
By Ezell's account, Anderson told him the test
had gone fine: they had watched it

96
00:06:16,080 --> 00:06:18,730
for 30 minutes, with no flow.

97
00:06:19,180 --> 00:06:23,850
At about 9:30, driller Dewey Revette noticed a
pressure difference, and the pumps

98
00:06:23,900 --> 00:06:25,210
were shut down.

99
00:06:25,260 --> 00:06:29,670
Ten minutes later, mud shot out of the
riser onto the rig floor.

100
00:06:30,520 --> 00:06:35,830
Gas was already above the blowout preventer,
rising and expanding up the riser.

101
00:06:35,880 --> 00:06:40,550
The crew sent the flow to a mud-gas separator on
the rig, rather than overboard, and

102
00:06:40,600 --> 00:06:42,070
closed the preventer.

103
00:06:42,120 --> 00:06:44,950
The separator was not built for a blowout.

104
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:47,330
Gas spread over the rig.

105
00:06:47,680 --> 00:06:52,630
Crane operator Micah Sandell saw mud shooting
all the way up the derrick, and then

106
00:06:52,680 --> 00:06:54,250
an explosion.

107
00:06:54,600 --> 00:06:58,210
Mike Williams was in his shop,
below the rig floor.

108
00:06:58,520 --> 00:07:01,090
[Williams] What happened next was I
heard a hissing noise

109
00:07:02,180 --> 00:07:03,510
and a thump.

110
00:07:03,860 --> 00:07:07,810
He heard alarms beeping from the
engine control room next door.

111
00:07:07,860 --> 00:07:10,030
Gas had reached the engines.

112
00:07:10,300 --> 00:07:14,830
[Williams] I could hear engine number three start
to rev up, and it's normal operating

113
00:07:15,520 --> 00:07:18,550
RPMs to way above what I'd ever
heard it run before.

114
00:07:18,730 --> 00:07:20,400
I reach down to grab my door,

115
00:07:22,010 --> 00:07:24,600
and at the simultaneously of
grabbing the handle,

116
00:07:25,910 --> 00:07:28,460
the engine goes to a level that is

117
00:07:30,370 --> 00:07:31,860
higher than I can even describe it.

118
00:07:31,910 --> 00:07:37,550
It's spinning so fast that it just stopped
spinning, and there was a huge explosion.

119
00:07:39,080 --> 00:07:41,490
It was about 9:49.

120
00:07:41,880 --> 00:07:46,370
[Williams] As I'm crawling through the ECR, I crawl
across the bodies of, of at least two men.

121
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:48,090
I don't know their condition.

122
00:07:48,140 --> 00:07:49,630
I'm trying to get them to respond.

123
00:07:49,680 --> 00:07:50,470
They're not responding.

124
00:07:50,520 --> 00:07:54,690
I assume they're dead and keep moving, because
I know that I'm in no condition to

125
00:07:54,740 --> 00:07:55,530
help them.

126
00:07:55,580 --> 00:07:57,430
He made it outside.

127
00:07:57,700 --> 00:08:00,930
[Williams] the, the hissing noise has now
turned into a full-blown roar,

128
00:08:01,980 --> 00:08:04,170
and I look at the doghouse
and the derrick.

129
00:08:04,440 --> 00:08:08,190
The doghouse is on fire and about half of the
derrick at this point is on fire.

130
00:08:10,040 --> 00:08:12,030
It was then I realized we had a blowout.

131
00:08:13,320 --> 00:08:17,490
Williams told investigators he never
heard the general alarm.

132
00:08:17,772 --> 00:08:21,302
[Investigator] Did you at any time
hear any alarm, uh,

133
00:08:22,072 --> 00:08:25,582
on the, that would indicate a
general muster or-

134
00:08:25,672 --> 00:08:26,610
[Williams] Never.

135
00:08:26,660 --> 00:08:31,997
Investigators found that the general alarm came
minutes after the first gas alarms.

136
00:08:32,047 --> 00:08:35,870
Dynamic positioning officer Andrea
Fleytas sent a Mayday.

137
00:08:36,220 --> 00:08:39,050
Two lifeboats left from the
front of the rig.

138
00:08:39,100 --> 00:08:43,370
Others were cut off, and jumped 50 to
70 feet into the water.

139
00:08:43,420 --> 00:08:47,030
Williams stayed with a group trying
to launch a life raft.

140
00:08:47,320 --> 00:08:51,670
[Williams] So there we set, three of us with
no life raft and no lifeboats.

141
00:08:51,890 --> 00:08:56,520
it was at that point that I informed the other
two individuals that we can stay here

142
00:08:56,570 --> 00:08:57,920
and die or we can jump.

143
00:08:58,270 --> 00:09:01,980
I remember telling a, a, a young woman that was
beside me that we're gonna have to

144
00:09:02,030 --> 00:09:03,030
run and jump.

145
00:09:03,180 --> 00:09:04,490
I said, "He just did it.

146
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:06,150
You've got to do it."

147
00:09:07,980 --> 00:09:11,430
She kept saying she couldn't do it, and I said,
"Well, watch me then." And I took

148
00:09:11,480 --> 00:09:12,810
off running, and I jumped.

149
00:09:13,260 --> 00:09:14,260
I've got oil,

150
00:09:15,080 --> 00:09:19,350
hydraulic fluid, gasoline, diesel, whatever it
is that's floating on the water is

151
00:09:19,400 --> 00:09:23,670
now burning my entire body, and I'm, I'm
now covered in this sludge.

152
00:09:23,850 --> 00:09:27,980
At that point, I started backstroking with the
one arm and one leg that would work

153
00:09:28,830 --> 00:09:30,940
until I remember feeling no pain.

154
00:09:30,990 --> 00:09:32,400
I remember feeling no heat

155
00:09:33,490 --> 00:09:36,020
and thinking that, uh, that
was it, I had died.

156
00:09:36,350 --> 00:09:37,980
And then I heard something
in the distance.

157
00:09:38,030 --> 00:09:42,190
I heard, "Over here, over here." I felt
something start lifting me out of the water

158
00:09:43,692 --> 00:09:45,502
Uh, a small orange

159
00:09:46,112 --> 00:09:51,162
rescue craft, small boat had, had grabbed me
and flipped me over into the boat.

160
00:09:51,480 --> 00:09:53,950
The rescue boat had come
from the Bankston.

161
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:57,710
Its crew had backed the vessel away from the
rig and launched the boat to pull

162
00:09:57,760 --> 00:09:59,490
people from the water.

163
00:09:59,782 --> 00:10:00,552
[Williams] He said, "I can't leave.

164
00:10:00,602 --> 00:10:01,592
There's more people in the water.

165
00:10:01,642 --> 00:10:06,181
I see more lights." So we went to the next
light that we saw in the water and

166
00:10:06,231 --> 00:10:10,762
retrieved that person, who was, uh, Andrea,
a dynamic position operator.

167
00:10:11,140 --> 00:10:14,750
Andrea Fleytas, the officer who
had sent the Mayday.

168
00:10:15,100 --> 00:10:19,157
All 115 survivors ended up
on the Bankston.

169
00:10:19,207 --> 00:10:23,770
The first Coast Guard helicopter arrived a
little after eleven p.m. Through the

170
00:10:23,820 --> 00:10:27,990
night, the most badly injured were
lifted off and flown to shore.

171
00:10:28,340 --> 00:10:31,437
At the muster, eleven men were missing.

172
00:10:31,487 --> 00:10:34,130
The Coast Guard searched for three days.

173
00:10:34,180 --> 00:10:35,950
They were never found.

174
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:43,550
In the minutes after the explosions, subsea
supervisor Chris Pleasant pressed the

175
00:10:43,600 --> 00:10:45,670
emergency disconnect on the bridge.

176
00:10:45,720 --> 00:10:49,670
It should have closed the blowout preventer's
blind shear ram, the blades meant to

177
00:10:49,720 --> 00:10:54,230
cut the drill pipe and seal the well, and
freed the rig from the riser.

178
00:10:54,280 --> 00:10:56,230
The lights on the panel changed.

179
00:10:56,280 --> 00:10:58,690
The rig did not disconnect.

180
00:10:59,040 --> 00:11:03,870
The preventer also had an automatic backup,
called the deadman, meant to close the

181
00:11:03,920 --> 00:11:08,350
blades on its own if power, communication, and
hydraulic pressure from the rig were

182
00:11:08,400 --> 00:11:09,310
lost.

183
00:11:09,360 --> 00:11:12,090
After the explosions, they were.

184
00:11:12,440 --> 00:11:16,203
Investigators did not fully agree
on what happened next.

185
00:11:16,253 --> 00:11:20,530
The Chemical Safety Board concluded that the
deadman most likely did fire that

186
00:11:20,600 --> 00:11:24,870
night, but that the drill pipe inside had
buckled and been pushed off center.

187
00:11:24,920 --> 00:11:29,170
The blades caught the pipe at its edge,
and could not seal the well.

188
00:11:29,520 --> 00:11:34,170
The forensic examination for the federal joint
investigation also found the pipe off

189
00:11:34,280 --> 00:11:38,670
center, but concluded that the blades most
likely closed two days later, when an

190
00:11:38,720 --> 00:11:41,130
underwater robot triggered them.

191
00:11:47,803 --> 00:11:51,940
And by the time the crew closed the preventer,
the commission noted, the flow may

192
00:11:51,990 --> 00:11:54,500
already have been too strong to stop.

193
00:11:54,550 --> 00:11:57,680
Earlier detection would have
improved the odds.

194
00:11:58,130 --> 00:12:02,240
The blowout preventer was raised from the
seafloor in September twenty ten, and

195
00:12:02,290 --> 00:12:04,060
taken as evidence.

196
00:12:04,910 --> 00:12:08,700
The Deepwater Horizon burned
for about 36 hours.

197
00:12:08,750 --> 00:12:12,120
On the morning of April 22, it sank.

198
00:12:12,470 --> 00:12:15,807
Oil flowed from the well for 87 days.

199
00:12:15,857 --> 00:12:20,100
The government estimated that 4.9
million barrels were released.

200
00:12:20,150 --> 00:12:24,240
A federal court later put the amount at
about four million barrels.

201
00:12:24,590 --> 00:12:28,140
A capping stack stopped the
flow on July 15.

202
00:12:28,190 --> 00:12:33,880
On September 19, after a relief well, the well
was declared permanently sealed.

203
00:12:34,230 --> 00:12:39,380
BP pleaded guilty to fourteen criminal counts,
including eleven counts of seaman's

204
00:12:39,430 --> 00:12:43,647
manslaughter, and agreed to pay $4
billion in that case.

205
00:12:43,697 --> 00:12:46,980
A federal court later found it
grossly negligent.

206
00:12:47,030 --> 00:12:50,960
Transocean pleaded guilty to a
Clean Water Act violation.

207
00:12:51,510 --> 00:12:55,700
The federal agency that had overseen
offshore drilling was broken up.

208
00:12:55,750 --> 00:13:00,560
New rules for blowout preventers and well
control followed in twenty sixteen.

209
00:13:01,410 --> 00:13:04,280
So why was the well judged safe?

210
00:13:04,330 --> 00:13:08,800
Because the test that should have caught the
problem was read as a success, with no

211
00:13:08,850 --> 00:13:13,020
standard for reading it, and with an
explanation that was later found to have no

212
00:13:13,330 --> 00:13:13,947
basis.

213
00:13:13,997 --> 00:13:18,940
In fact, investigators concluded, the
cement at the bottom had failed.

214
00:13:19,290 --> 00:13:21,987
And why didn't the blowout
preventer stop it?

215
00:13:22,037 --> 00:13:25,213
The signs were missed until gas
was already above it.

216
00:13:25,263 --> 00:13:29,160
When its blades closed, the drill pipe was in
the wrong place for them to seal the

217
00:13:29,210 --> 00:13:30,260
well.

218
00:13:30,810 --> 00:13:35,800
The commission traced the disaster to a series
of mistakes by BP, Halliburton, and

219
00:13:35,850 --> 00:13:38,973
Transocean, and to failures of management.

220
00:13:39,023 --> 00:13:43,700
It also wrote that the exact part each
mistake played may never be known.
